#edb/ref 1072 #uuid "630a0699-f234-53c6-a49d-ff388f9bf008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088266 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088266 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088266 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f123b77781ae6c6665482418480605e8c61455d637dd92b8c929fa8ace7da3e.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function given above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94dd0256-e111-5129-838c-0c9af377631d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088716 #edb/ref 1061 3591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "044f45f9-5af9-501f-9a03-a9fbc7e5fe3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088717 #edb/ref 1061 3594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9250adfb-c077-551c-863b-fcfec3c5f707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088718 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088718 #edb/ref 1061 3595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8bbb903a-a04c-56a6-acf5-3a222f28877b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088719 #edb/ref 1061 3599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b163c36-e16f-5816-89bc-31e6d0e6d2e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088720 #edb/ref 1061 36132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6223949-e2a0-5ff7-b6ee-50b71083e049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088721 #edb/ref 1061 36133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f02d327c-832e-5332-9eb5-faf50ec5e5ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088722 #edb/ref 1061 36159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7745216e-b663-5416-b6ab-551281585ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088723 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088723 #edb/ref 1061 36160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b286237-0bb2-5745-9955-20dfeb247f45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088865 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2660977b21e476e1a295e215ac22888625763b12b1685521c289adf8326f78c.png)\n\nNo matter which number $x$ we pick as an input, $f(x)$ always will return an output. Therefore, we can write the domain of this function as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nshown below:\n\n![](images/84/84fefed3d54e57467ca3ebcf823c6bbf5c3c6aacce2f7542230b1abacfd97f46.png)\n\nWe cannot substitute the value $x=0$ into the function $g(x)$ because division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not part of the domain of $g(x)$. However, we can use any other number $x$ as input for $g(x)$. Therefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty, 0)\\cup (0,\\infty)\n$$\n\nThis notation means that any number *apart from zero* can be used as an input for $g(x)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088865 #edb/ref 1065 1489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088865 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9b55c3b-309c-593d-bfdc-2164ed4cf232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088865 #edb/ref 1067 "The Domain of a Function Given as a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088866 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the function $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2a/2a666cbc79869f13f7079db92396ae6f7ad2b2041b2aa0ea704149119a850eed.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0$, nor is it defined for $x > 2$.\n- The function has filled circles at $x=0$ and $x=2$, so it is defined at both of these points.\n- In addition, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWe can also express the domain using interval notation:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,2]\n$$\n\nThe square brackets $[\\:]$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are included in the domain." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088866 #edb/ref 1065 1490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088866 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7454c76-12f4-56da-b55f-0fcbaed1c9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088866 #edb/ref 1067 "Functions Defined on Closed Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088981 #edb/ref 1064 "The **domain** of a function is the set of all input values for which it is defined.\n\nFor example, consider the function represented by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c39c6cdd67f1b69248b38e009b6de12acef250a59543aca2d2922ec1801d57.png)\n\nThe domain corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set. Therefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a situation where a function is defined using a table." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088981 #edb/ref 1065 5909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088981 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e8b5c9de-7970-5fcf-b242-aa035f6c2492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088981 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088982 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider the function $y=g(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c99d3cbb1b7ba516cc2cf75856aeb17b9f91d23185d6fd25319a492ca4e3b164.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0$, nor is it defined for $x > 2$.\n- The function has open circles at $x=0$ and $x=2$, so it is *not* defined at either of these points.\n- However, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x \\lt 2\n$$\n\nwhich we can write using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in (0,2)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\:)$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are excluded from the domain." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088982 #edb/ref 1065 5919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088982 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f49de54b-2bd4-5ee3-9e87-ca07573dea27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088982 #edb/ref 1067 "Functions Defined on Open Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088983 #edb/ref 1064 "It's possible for a function to have one endpoint included in the domain and another endpoint excluded.\n\nFor example, consider the function is $y=h(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0919c9b416ad49ed08d566b8c85d250a1a00a3ebf72f4d22c3d608fb42ede169.png)\n\nThe endpoint $x=-2$ is included in the domain, but the endpoint $x=2$ is excluded. Therefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x \\lt 2\n$$\n\nwhich we can write using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in[-2, 2)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088983 #edb/ref 1065 5920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088983 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "85e2582d-5cd6-558b-8925-e538db397f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088983 #edb/ref 1067 "Functions Defined on Semi-Open Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
